# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `articleflux serve` reads .env from the directory it is started in, before it
# parses its flags, so everything here is a DEFAULT. Two rules follow from that
# and both matter:
#
#   * A flag always wins over this file.  (`-addr` beats ARTICLEFLUX_ADDR)
#   * The real environment always wins over this file. A systemd unit sets its
#     own environment through EnvironmentFile=, and a stale .env sitting in a
#     deployment directory must never be able to override it.
#
# An empty value means "not set" — a copied-but-unfilled line falls back to the
# built-in default rather than blanking it.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development: no login
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ARTICLEFLUX_DEV=1 is the same switch as `serve -dev`: the server hands every
# request the local account with NO LOGIN AT ALL, and the client goes straight
# to the reader without a password prompt. It is what `make dev` and
# `./scripts/make.ps1 dev` are for.
#
# It is refused in the two cases where it would be a hole rather than a
# convenience, and the refusal is loud rather than silent:
#
#   * on any bind that is not loopback, and
#   * together with -behind-proxy — a proxy in front of a loopback bind is a
#     published instance, which is exactly the case this must never apply to.
#
# Set it to 1 only on the machine you are developing on. If you ever copy a .env
# to a server, delete this line first.
ARTICLEFLUX_DEV=

# The local account -dev creates on first run.
#
# You should not have to type these: on a LOOPBACK origin the login screen
# prefills both fields with them, so signing in is one click. That prefill never
# happens on a deployed instance — it is gated on the page origin being
# 127.0.0.1 / localhost / [::1], which no server response can influence.
#
# The password is 15 characters because `init`, `adduser` and `passwd` enforce a
# 12-character minimum, and a documented default that those commands reject is a
# documented default that wastes an afternoon.
#
# They are the built-in defaults, written out here so nobody has to read main.go
# to find out what the dev password is. They are not secrets and are not meant to
# be — an instance anyone else can reach must not be in dev mode at all. On a
# real deployment the first account comes from `articleflux init`, which prompts
# for a password and never writes it anywhere.
ARTICLEFLUX_DEV_USER=cam
ARTICLEFLUX_DEV_PASSWORD=articleflux-dev


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Serving
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Defaults shown. Loopback, because nothing about this server should face the
# internet without a reverse proxy terminating TLS in front of it — see
# deploy/README.md.
ARTICLEFLUX_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9000
ARTICLEFLUX_WEB=bin/web

# Exact page origins allowed to open the gRPC tunnel, comma-separated, e.g.
# https://reader.example.com — no trailing slash. Empty falls back to the
# WebSocket library's same-origin policy, which compares Origin against Host and
# therefore depends on the proxy forwarding Host faithfully. Production sets this
# explicitly rather than trusting someone else's nginx.
ARTICLEFLUX_ORIGIN=

# Trust X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP for client addresses. ONLY set this when a
# proxy you control is in front: it is a request header, so any client can send
# one, and trusting it unconditionally lets anybody write whatever address they
# like into your log. Setting it also refuses ARTICLEFLUX_DEV, on purpose.
ARTICLEFLUX_BEHIND_PROXY=

# Re-serve article images through this server instead of letting the browser
# fetch them from the publisher (plan.md §10.1a).
#
# ON by default — the only setting here that is. The server can reach a
# publisher your own network may block, and when it does the article text
# arrives while every image hangs, which from the reading pane is
# indistinguishable from this application being broken. Turning it off is a real
# choice with a real cost on the other side: every image becomes an outbound
# request from this box and a cache entry on its disk.
#
# Set to 0/false/no/off to disable for the instance; individual readers can opt
# out with the `proxy.images` preference.
ARTICLEFLUX_PROXY_IMAGES=

# Absolute origin for proxied content, e.g. https://proxy.example.com.
#
# Empty means proxy URLs are same-origin and relative, which is correct for
# images and NOT correct for the tier-2 page proxy that is coming: proxied HTML
# must never share an origin with the app holding your session (§10.1b, D20).
# Set this before the page proxy ships and the split costs nothing; set it after
# and it is a migration of every URL already minted.
ARTICLEFLUX_PROXY_ORIGIN=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Administration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Read by `init`, `adduser` and `passwd` when -password is not given, so a
# provisioning script need not put a password on a command line where it lands in
# shell history and in `ps` output. Left empty here: these commands prompt on a
# terminal, which is the better path for a human.
ARTICLEFLUX_PASSWORD=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Smart+ voice (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ArticleFlux runs completely without this. The only thing it unlocks is the Smart+
# voice (plan.md A31, §10.7) — the browser's own speech synthesiser reads
# articles for free, offline, with no key at all, and that is the default.
#
# Smart+ sends the text of the article being read to OpenAI. It is off per user
# until they turn it on, AND the server refuses to egress at all without a key,
# so an instance that never sets one cannot send anything anywhere.
#
# This is now the FALLBACK rather than the only way. Settings > Smart+ stores a
# key in the database, encrypted, and a key saved there takes over from this one
# — so the usual path is to leave this empty and paste the key into the running
# app. A key set here is still honoured, and the settings screen says so, because
# "I deleted the key and it still works" is otherwise unexplainable.
#
# Every Smart+ feature spends this one key: the voice (§10.7) and the interface
# translation (§10.5a) both read it.
OPENAI_API_KEY=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets at rest (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The key that seals stored credentials — today the Smart+ API key, later the
# mailbox passwords the IMAP poller has to be able to USE and therefore cannot
# hash. Leave it empty and the server generates one at `secrets.key` beside the
# database, mode 0600, on first run.
#
# What that buys, stated plainly: it protects a leaked DATABASE — a backup, a
# `VACUUM INTO` copy, a .db someone emailed themselves. It does NOT protect
# against a compromised host, because anything that can read the database can
# read the file next to it. Set this instead when your deployment keeps keys
# somewhere the disk is not (an EnvironmentFile on tmpfs, a secrets manager).
#
# Exactly 32 raw bytes. In practice that means reading it from a file rather
# than typing it. LOSING IT MAKES EVERY STORED SECRET PERMANENTLY UNREADABLE,
# and the failure is quiet — the settings screen will say "not configured"
# about a key that is sitting right there.
ARTICLEFLUX_SECRET_KEY=
